  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nBy Tuesday, the Omega Block in place across the CONUS will begin to \nbreak down with an upper-level ridge over the Central Plains \nshifting back eastward as flow becomes more progressive, inducing \nlow-level south/southwesterly WAA with 850-hPa temperatures \nreturning to between 21 and 23 C (95-99th climatological \npercentile). This process will result in high temperatures in the \nlow to mid-90s F, but humidity/dewpoints will be much slower to \nincrease and keep heat index values closer to air temperatures.\n\nA potent upper-level trough tracking across southern Canada will \ndrive a deepening surface cyclone toward Hudson Bay on Wednesday \nwith a trailing cold front reaching the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley. The timing of this front in model guidance continues to \ntrend ever-so-slightly earlier, bringing Wednesday's temperatures \nmore into question. Showers and thunderstorms are also expected to \nbe ongoing along and moreso behind the front with the potential for \nsome clouds to spread into the warm sector. Since the general \nconsensus is for the front to reach the I-70 corridor by early \nafternoon, the hottest temperatures mainly in the mid to upper 90s F \nare forecast along and south. This trend is less supportive of \nrecord-breaking high temperatures around 100 F, but increasing and \npooled dewpoints near the front still favors afternoon heat index \nvalues of 100 to 105 F with the potential for a Heat Advisory (105+ \nF) needing to be weighed going forward.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will accompany the cold front as it slowly \nadvances southward through the CWA Wednesday into early Thursday, \nsupported by 80 to 90+ percent probabilities of measurable rainfall \nin ensemble model guidance that are focused on Wednesday afternoon \ninto Thursday morning. Probabilities of 0.5\" and 1\" of rainfall also \ncontinue to increase near the I-70 and I-44 corridors, reaching \naround 60 and 20 percent, respectively. Deep-layer wind shear is \nindicated to be 25 to 35 kt accompanied by 60 to 80 percent \nprobabilities of 1000+ J/kg MUCAPE, suggesting there is at least a \nlow threat of a few strong to severe thunderstorms Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. However, a capping inversion across the warm \nsector and strongest forcing and wind shear lagging behind the front \ndecreases confidence in thunderstorms being favorably surface based. \nShowers and clouds will gradually end from north to south on \nThursday morning into midday with a cooler airmass promoting high \ntemperatures in the low to mid-80s F with upper 70s F not out of the \nquestion across portions of southeastern MO/southwestern IL if \nprecipitation and thicker cloud cover can linger there into \nafternoon.\n\nModel guidance continues to disagree on the position and movement of \nan amplifying upper-level ridge across the CONUS into the weekend, \nsplit largely into two camps highlighted by cluster analyses: one \nwhere the ridge migrates eastward over the Mid-Mississippi River \nValley with hot, well-above average temperatures returning; and one \nwhere the ridge remains positioned to the west or southwest with \ntemperatures remaining slightly above average. There is at least \nsome potential for showers and thunderstorms in either solution as \none or more shortwave troughs navigate the ridge in the former \nsolution or northwesterly flow in the latter. However, ensemble \nmodel members, although widely varying, have any periods of \nprecipitation being transient with most of the period being dry \nrather than wet.",
